      "@id": "urn:adk-prov:runs/data/8cdc60bf3d744f0aa54b82256fa60a71",
      "@type": "lcont:LLMResponse",
      "dcterms:title": "response:ProcessDescriptor_agent",
      "prov:value": "```json\n{\n  \"success\": \"Industrial Process Description: Alkylation of Ethyl Acetoacetate with Butyl Bromide\\n\\n**REACTION OVERVIEW:**\\nThis is a nucleophilic substitution reaction (SN2/alkylation) where the active methylene carbon of ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated by butyl bromide to form ethyl 2-acetylhexanoate.\\n\\n**PROCESS DETAILS:**\\n\\n1. **REAGENT PREPARATION:**\\n   - Ethyl acetoacetate (CH3COCH2COOC2H5): ~99.5% purity\\n   - Butyl bromide (C4H9Br): ~99% purity\\n   - Molar ratio typically 1:1.2 to 1:1.5 (excess alkyl halide to drive completion)\\n\\n2. **CATALYTIC/BASIC CONDITIONS:**\\n   - Base required: Potassium carbonate (K2CO3), sodium ethoxide (NaOC2H5), or sodium hydride (NaH) is commonly used\\n   - Base converts acetoacetate to its enolate form, generating the nucleophile\\n   - Typical base loading: 1.0-1.5 equivalents\\n\\n3. **SOLVENT SYSTEM:**\\n   - Aprotic polar solvents preferred for SN2 reactivity\\n   - Dimethylformamide (DMF), dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), or acetone commonly used\\n   - Solvent volume: typically 3-5 volumes relative to main reactant\\n\\n4. **REACTION CONDITIONS:**\\n   - Temperature: 60-80°C (optimal balance between reaction rate and selectivity)\\n   - Reaction time: 4-8 hours with stirring\\n   - Pressure: atmospheric (or slightly elevated if using sealed reactor)\\n   - No external catalyst needed beyond base\\n\\n5. **ENERGY REQUIREMENTS:**\\n   - Endothermic activation required: ~60-80°C heating\\n   - Exothermic reaction release: moderate (~50-100 kJ/mol)\\n   - Net requirement: continuous heating to maintain temperature\\n\\n6. **EQUIPMENT SETUP:**\\n   - 500-5000L jacketed reactor with mechanical stirring\\n   - Reflux condenser or Dean-Stark trap if using volatile solvents\\n   - Thermometer, addition funnel for controlled reagent addition\\n   - Inert atmosphere (N2) maintained to prevent side reactions\\n\\n7. **PROCESS WORKFLOW:**\\n   - Charge base and solvent to reactor\\n   - Add ethyl acetoacetate slowly with stirring under N2\\n   - Warm to 60-70°C\\n   - Dropwise addition of butyl bromide over 1-2 hours\\n   - Maintain temperature for 4-6 hours post-addition\\n   - Cool to ambient temperature\\n\\n8. **BYPRODUCT & SEPARATION:**\\n   - Primary byproduct: HBr (gas) and bromide salt (in solution)\\n   - Minor side products: potential dialkylated product (over-alkylation) if excess alkyl halide used\\n   - Workup: quench with water or dilute HCl, extract with ethyl acetate\\n   - Remove salts via aqueous wash\\n   - Solvent removal via vacuum distillation\\n   - Product isolated by column chromatography if high purity needed\\n\\n9. **YIELD & SELECTIVITY:**\\n   - Expected yield: 70-85% under standard conditions\\n   - Selectivity: monoalkylation favored over dialkylation with controlled stoichiometry\\n   - E/Z selectivity not applicable (saturated product)\\n\\n10. **SPECIAL CONSIDERATIONS:**\\n    - Halide (Br-) is a poor leaving group in this context; the reaction relies on activated methylene reactivity\\n    - HBr generation can promote side reactions; pH control recommended\\n    - Moisture sensitivity: maintain anhydrous conditions\\n    - Temperature control critical to prevent decomposition and unwanted condensations\\n    - Scale-up requires careful heat management due to exothermic nature\\n\\n11. **INDUSTRIAL VARIANTS:**\\n    - Phase-transfer catalysis (PTC) using quaternary ammonium salts for aqueous-organic systems\\n    - Continuous flow reactors for better heat control and safety\\n    - In situ base generation to reduce storage of hazardous bases\\n\\n**KEY PROCESS PARAMETERS:**\\n- Conversion: typically >90% at complete reaction time\\n- Atom economy: ~77% (considering Br- as byproduct)\\n- Green chemistry metrics: solvent-intensive; consider recovery/recycling\"\n}\n```",
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
