    {
      "@id": "urn:adk-prov:runs/data/f50529966d5c4e859d55d2e495de0d45",
      "@type": "lcont:LLMResponse",
      "dcterms:title": "response:ProcessDescriptor_agent",
      "prov:value": "```json\n{\n  \"success\": \"Industrial Hydroformylation and Reduction Process for 2-Ethylhexanol Production\",\n  \"process_details\": {\n    \"reaction_type\": \"Hydroformylation followed by reduction\",\n    \"overall_transformation\": \"2-butylbutane-1,3-diol + CO → 2-ethylhexan-1-ol + CO₂\",\n    \"note\": \"The reaction equation appears simplified; the actual industrial process involves hydroformylation chemistry\",\n    \n    \"industrial_process\": {\n      \"primary_application\": \"Production of 2-ethylhexanol (2-EH), a major industrial plasticizer alcohol precursor\",\n      \n      \"typical_conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"150-200°C\",\n        \"pressure\": \"200-300 psi (CO partial pressure: 50-100 psi)\",\n        \"solvent\": \"Organic solvent (typically the diol itself or aromatic hydrocarbons)\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cobalt or rhodium-based carbonyl catalysts (HCo(CO)₄ or RhH(CO)(PPh₃)₃)\",\n        \"hydrogen_source\": \"Syngas (CO + H₂) or external H₂ stream for reductive steps\"\n      },\n      \n      \"process_steps\": {\n        \"step_1\": \"Hydroformylation of the secondary alcohol using CO and catalyst to form aldehyde intermediate\",\n        \"step_2\": \"In-situ or subsequent reduction of aldehyde to primary alcohol using H₂\",\n        \"step_3\": \"Catalyst separation and product workup\",\n        \"byproduct_formation\": \"CO₂ likely forms from oxidation side reactions or water-assisted CO conversion\"\n      },\n      \n      \"catalytic_system\": {\n        \"cobalt_process\": \"Lower cost, requires higher pressure (250-300 psi), produces linear:branched ratio ~3:1\",\n        \"rhodium_process\": \"Higher selectivity, lower pressure capable, better regioselectivity for desired 2-EH isomer\",\n        \"phosphine_ligands\": \"Common to enhance selectivity (PPh₃, TPPTS for aqueous systems)\"\n      },\n      \n      \"reactor_type\": \"Fixed-bed or bubble column reactor with gas-liquid contact optimization\",\n      \n      \"energy_requirements\": {\n        \"thermal_energy\": \"High (process endothermic in hydroformylation; exothermic in reduction)\",\n        \"hydrogen_source\": \"~1-2 mol H₂ per mol product (if using syngas)\",\n        \"total_CO_required\": \"~1 mol per mol feedstock\"\n      },\n      \n      \"separation_downstream\": {\n        \"catalyst_recovery\": \"Extraction/distillation to separate volatile products; catalyst recycling critical for economics\",\n        \"product_purification\": \"Distillation to obtain >95% 2-ethylhexanol\",\n        \"byproduct_handling\": \"CO₂ separated and vented or recovered\"\n      },\n      \n      \"industrial_relevance\": \"2-Ethylhexanol is precursor to di(2-ethylhexyl)phthalate (DEHP), one of the largest volume plasticizers globally\",\n      \n      \"key_challenges\": {\n        \"selectivity\": \"Controlling regioselectivity to maximize linear vs. branched product\",\n        \"catalyst_efficiency\": \"Long catalyst lifetime and cost-effectiveness critical\",\n        \"pressure_safety\": \"High-pressure operation requires robust equipment\",\n        \"heat_removal\": \"Managing exothermic reduction step\"\n      },\n      \n      \"alternative_feedstock_note\": \"Commercially, 2-EH is more commonly produced from propylene hydroformylation pathway rather than from a diol precursor as shown here\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```",
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "urn:adk-prov:runs/prompt/ProcessDescriptor_agent_process_completion_d41d8cd98f00b204"
      },
      "prov:wasGeneratedBy": {
        "@id": "urn:adk-prov:runs/process/cccf0e718ec947e7a00bad4437ebead4"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:adk-prov:runs/prompt/orchestrator_agent_synthesis_route_completion_06f5bc3cc98bcdd9",
      "@type": "lcont:Prompt",
      "dcterms:title": "prompt:orchestrator_agent",
      "prov:value": {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "SYSTEM:\n# ORCHESTRATOR\n\nYou are an agent orchestrator for Life Cycle Inventory construction. You do not interact with humans, be concise.\n\n## Key Rules\n- DO NOT use tools not listed in the current task's instructions — each task specifies exactly which tools are allowed\n- If a sub-agent gives wrong answers, call it again with the error details and more precise instructions\n- **NEVER use `go_to_task` to rewind to a task earlier than the current one unless the current task explicitly requires it** — rewinding does not fix validation errors\n- Do NOT proceed to the next task without finishing the current one\n\n## When a sub-agent call is blocked (cap exhausted)\nIf you receive a message that a sub-agent has been blocked because its call cap is exhausted:\n- **Stop calling that agent immediately** — all further calls will be blocked\n- Do NOT loop on `load_json` or any other tool — this will not unblock the capped agent\n\n## save_json format\nThe `file` argument must be a serialized JSON string where every array element is a JSON **object**, never a nested JSON string.\n- Correct: `{{\"nodes\": [{{\"reaction_SMILES\": \"...\", ...}}]}}`\n- Wrong:   `{{\"nodes\": [\"{{\\\"reaction_SMILES\\\": \\\"...\\\"}}\"]}}`  — strings inside the array break schema validation\n\n## Your current task is:\n\n## SYNTHESIS ROUTE COMPLETION\n\nLoad the retrosynthesis route by calling: `load_json(\"/home/cogoni/Workspace/PINK/pink-chemlcibuilder/data/outputs/json/Octocrylene/74268b26/retros/retro_0.json\")`.\n**Use this exact path — do not reconstruct, shorten, or modify it in any way.**\nCount the total number of nodes in the loaded JSON (the `nodes` array length). **You must process every single node — including deep branches.**\n\nThe retrosynthesis tree may have multiple branches with many levels. A reaction at the root produces the target; its precursor nodes are further reactions that produce the inputs to the root, and so on. Every node at every depth is a real reaction that must be completed.\n\nFor each reaction step in the retrosynthesis route:\n1. Call the 'SynthesisCompleter_agent' with the \"reaction_smiles\" entry **This is your only subagent for this task**\n2. Collect all completed reaction steps into a flat list under the \"nodes\" key\n3. DO NOT nest reactions - every step is a separate entry regardless of depth\n4. Leave \"node_id\", \"precursor_node_ids\", and \"root_node_id\" empty - they are filled automatically\n\n**Before saving, verify that the number of completed nodes equals the total node count you recorded from the retrosynthesis JSON. If any nodes are missing, go back and call SynthesisCompleter_agent for the missing reaction_smiles entries.**\n\n## Output Format\nFollow this structure:\n[output JSON schema omitted]"
      }
    },
